Feature Selection & Engineering

Feature Engineering was done by creating a new feature, Average Score, to represent the overall academic performance of each student which helped in studying the patterns of student overall performance with all independent features.
Selecting Features for train test split:
On basis of correlation of features the relevant features were picked for building model.
X(Independent Variable)   Gender, Parental Education, Lunch, Test Preparation, Race/Ethnicity, Reading Score and Writing Score.
Y(Target) Math Score
Model Development

Three regression algorithms were implemented:
•	Linear Regression 
•	Decision Tree Regressor 
•	Random Forest Regressor 
The dataset was divided into training and testing sets using an 80:20 split, and model performance was evaluated using Mean Squared Error (MSE), Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE), and the coefficient of determination (R² Score).
Linear Regression serves as the baseline model. Decision Tree captures nonlinear patterns. Random Forest improves prediction accuracy through ensemble learning.


Results and Discussion
Models are compared using regression metrics. Random Forest generally provides the highest predictive performance, while Linear Regression offers better interpretability.


Key Findings
•	Students who completed the test preparation course generally achieved higher average scores. 
•	Reading and writing scores exhibited a strong positive correlation. 
•	Female students demonstrated slightly higher average performance than male students. 
•	Lunch type and parental education level showed measurable associations with student achievement. 
•	The absence of significant outliers indicated that no additional outlier treatment as required. 
